-- allium: 3
--
-- JEBOL: the REBOL 3 (R3-Alpha) value model.
--
-- This module specifies what a REBOL value IS: the datatypes, how series
-- values share mutable storage, how words carry bindings, and when two
-- values count as equal. It specifies no evaluation; see eval.allium.
--
-- Some datatypes here are names and nothing more. `#(vector!)` is a datatype
-- value and `datatype? #(vector!)` is true, because the reader has no
-- business deciding which datatypes exist -- but no value of one can be made,
-- so there is nothing to specify beyond the name. Eight of them: library!,
-- routine!, vector!, command!, task!, utype!, rebcode! and frame!.
--
-- struct! is neither a full datatype nor a bare name. A value of it can be
-- made and STRUCT? affirms it, but only the layout is held. StructValue below
-- says what that leaves out and why.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every datatype JEBOL represents in milestone 1. The names match the
-- REBOL spelling with the trailing "!" dropped, because "!" is not a
-- legal character in an Allium enum literal.
enum Datatype {
    unset | none | logic | integer | decimal | percent | money | char
    | pair | tuple | time | date
    | string | file | url | email | tag | binary | ref
    | word | set_word | get_word | lit_word | refinement | issue
    | block | paren | path | set_path | get_path | lit_path
    | datatype | native | function | op | object | error | map
    | module | port -- a context that carries something extra: a header, or
                    -- a way out of the interpreter
    | java_object   -- a value belonging to the host, reachable both ways

    -- Named but not built. No value of these exists yet, and the names
    -- still have to: `#(bitset!)` is a datatype value and `datatype?`
    -- answers true for it, whether or not a bitset can be made.
    --
    -- This was tried once as a way to stop the reader refusing `bitset!`
    -- in source and reverted, because that was the wrong fix -- a bare
    -- name is a word and the reader has no business deciding which
    -- datatypes exist. The names are needed for the construction form,
    -- which is a different question and does need them.
    | action | bitset | closure | command | end | event | frame | gob
    | handle | hash | image | library | rebcode
    | struct | task | utype | vector
}

-- The concrete datatypes reachable through each family variant. A family
-- exists where several datatypes share one representation and differ only
-- in how the loader spells them and how MOLD prints them.
enum StringDatatype { string | file | url | email | tag | ref }
enum BlockDatatype { block | paren | path | set_path | get_path | lit_path }
enum WordDatatype { word | set_word | get_word | lit_word | refinement | issue }

-- Error categories as R3-Alpha groups them in its error catalogue.
-- The `throw` category is what a control-flow signal looks like once
-- TRY/ALL has turned it into a value. Confirmed against a real R3:
-- `try/all [throw 5]` gives type Throw, id throw, and a stray break gives
-- type Throw, id break. Nothing raises one directly.
enum ErrorCategory { syntax | script | math | access | user | internal | throw }

-- How a function parameter consumes its argument.
--
-- The two quoting kinds are named for how firmly they hold, because
-- naming them "literal" and "soft literal" left nothing to say which
-- sigil was which, and they were specified the wrong way round until a
-- real R3 was asked. The sigil is now part of each name's explanation.
enum ParameterKind {
    normal          -- evaluates the argument
    | hard_quoted   -- takes the argument exactly as written (a :word in the spec)
    | soft_quoted   -- as written (a 'word in the spec) unless the caller opts out
    | refinement    -- a /refinement switch, contributing logic true or none
    | refinement_arg-- an argument belonging to the preceding refinement
    | return_type   -- a return: annotation, contributing no argument
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A parameter of a function's interface, derived from its spec block.
--
-- An empty accepted_types is not "no constraint". A spec word written with
-- no type block beside it accepts every value except unset, which is what
-- the C gives it -- `TS_VALUE`, every datatype but unset. A parameter that
-- really takes unset writes `any-type!` in its spec, and that arrives here
-- as the full member set rather than as emptiness.
--
-- So emptiness carries meaning and has to be read, not skipped over. The
-- acceptance rule is ABareParameterAcceptsAnyValueButUnset in eval.allium.
value Parameter {
    name: String
    kind: ParameterKind
    accepted_types: Set<Datatype>
    owning_refinement: String?
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Mutable storage shared by every series value that points into it.
-- Two series values that share storage see each other's mutations; this
-- is the property that makes REBOL series aliasing observable.

entity BlockStorage {
    items: List<Value>

    length: items.count
}

entity StringStorage {
    codepoints: List<Integer>

    length: codepoints.count
}

entity BinaryStorage {
    bytes: List<Integer>

    length: bytes.count

    invariant BytesAreOctets {
        for byte in bytes:
            byte >= 0 and byte <= 255
    }
}

-- An image's pixels, four bytes each, with the shape they are arranged in.
--
-- A series like any other, and that is the whole trick of it: `IMG_WIDE` and
-- `IMG_HIGH` sit beside the data, the element is a quad -- `QUAD_SKIP(s, n)` is
-- `data + n * 4` -- and the tail is `w * h`. So a position into an image is a
-- pixel index, and every navigation action an image has comes free from that.
--
-- The bytes are red, green, blue and alpha in that order, on every platform.
-- Rebol's own order is not: `include/reb-c.h` picks ARGB on a big-endian host,
-- RGBA on Android and BGRA elsewhere, so the same image is laid out three ways
-- depending on where Rebol was built. What a script sees is fixed either way --
-- molding writes `RRGGBB` and a pixel is the tuple `r.g.b.a` -- so the fixed
-- order is the language and the varying one is an implementation detail. See
-- decision 20: JEBOL is meant for desktop, the web and Android, and Android is
-- the platform Rebol treats differently.
--
-- Alpha is stored for every pixel whether the image uses one or not, which is why
-- molding asks `Image_Has_Alpha` before writing the second binary rather than
-- reading a flag.
--
-- Width times height is the pixel count and neither may pass 0xFFFF:
-- `if (w > 0xFFFF || h > 0xFFFF)` refuses, with `size-limit` when MAKE asked and
-- silently when a codec did.
entity ImageStorage {
    pixels: List<Integer>
    wide: Integer
    high: Integer

    length: wide * high

    invariant FourBytesAPixel {
        pixels.count = wide * high * 4
    }

    invariant PixelBytesAreOctets {
        for byte in pixels:
            byte >= 0 and byte <= 255
    }

    invariant NeitherSideIsNegative {
        wide >= 0 and high >= 0
    }

    invariant NeitherSideIsBiggerThanTheLimit {
        wide <= 65535 and high <= 65535
    }
}

-- What a gob! holds: a place on a screen, one piece of content, and children.
--
-- Two things about it are not object-like, and both come from `Set_GOB_Var`.
--
-- The content is a UNION rather than five fields. `image`, `draw`, `text`,
-- `effect` and `color` all write `GOB_CONTENT` and set a type tag beside it, so
-- giving a gob an image takes away whatever draw block it had. Reading the one it
-- has not got answers none.
--
-- And the pane is a series. Every navigation and modification action a gob has --
-- 24 of them in `REBTYPE(Gob)` -- works on the list of children rather than on
-- the gob itself: `append gob child` puts a child in, `length? gob` counts them,
-- and `pick gob 1` is the first child. A gob with no pane answers none to PICK
-- and zero to LENGTH?, because the pane is allocated when the first child
-- arrives.
--
-- A fresh gob is not an empty one. `Make_Gob` clears the struct and then writes
-- three fields: `GOB_W(gob) = 100; GOB_H(gob) = 100; GOB_ALPHA(gob) = 255;`. So
-- `make gob! []` is a hundred by a hundred and fully opaque, and the offset is
-- the only field that starts at nothing.
entity GobStorage {
    offset: PairValue             -- where it sits, in float pixels; starts 0x0
    size: PairValue               -- starts 100x100, not 0x0
    alpha: Integer                -- 0 to 255, clipped on the way in; starts 255
    content_kind: GobContent
    content: Value?               -- the one piece the kind names
    pane: List<Value>             -- child gobs, and the series every arm walks
    parent: Value?
    data_kind: GobHeld            -- a second tag, not part of the content union
    data: Value?
    owner: Value?                 -- written and never read
    flags: List<GobFlag>

    length: pane.count

    invariant AlphaIsAnOctet {
        alpha >= 0 and alpha <= 255
    }

    invariant ContentMatchesItsKind {
        content_kind = none implies content = absent
    }

    invariant DataMatchesItsKind {
        data_kind = nothing implies data = absent
    }
}

-- The kinds of content a gob can hold, one at a time. `GOBT_*` in reb-gob.h.
--
-- Two of these read back through one field name: a block written to `text` is
-- `text` and a string written to it is `string`, and `g/text` answers whichever
-- went in.
enum GobContent {
    none | color | image | draw | text | string | effect | widget
}

-- The kinds of thing `data` holds. `GOBD_*`, and a tag of its own, which is what
-- lets one gob carry a draw block and a block of data at the same time.
enum GobHeld {
    nothing | object | block | string | binary | integer
}

-- The flag words `flags` accepts, from `Set_Gob_Flag`.
--
-- Nine of them, in this order, because `Flags_To_Block` walks the same table to
-- build the block a script reads: `g/flags` comes back in table order whatever
-- order it was written in.
enum GobFlag {
    resize | no_title | no_border | dropable | transparent
    | popup | modal | on_top | hidden
}

-- A binding target: a set of named slots that words can resolve through.
-- The global environment and every object! share this representation.

entity Context {
    parent: Context?
    slots: ContextSlot with context = this

    slot_count: slots.count
}

-- Key-to-value storage for a map!, mutable and shared exactly as series
-- storage is. Kept apart from Context because a map's keys are values
-- rather than names: #[1 2] has the integer 1 as a key, and nothing about
-- a map is a binding target.
entity MapStorage {
    entries: List<MapEntry>

    length: entries.count

    invariant KeysAreUnique {
        for entry in entries:
            entries.count(other => other.key = entry.key) = 1
    }
}

value MapEntry {
    key: Value
    value: Value
}

entity ContextSlot {
    context: Context
    spelling: String            -- as first written, case preserved
    canonical: String           -- lowercased, the form used for lookup
    value: Value
    is_protected: Boolean

    invariant CanonicalIsLowercase {
        canonical = lowercase(spelling)
    }
}

-- The value itself. Every REBOL datatype is one variant of this sum type.

entity Value {
    kind: UnsetValue | NoneValue | LogicValue | IntegerValue | DecimalValue
        | MoneyValue | CharValue | PairValue | TupleValue | TimeValue | DateValue
        | AnyStringValue | BinaryValue | AnyBlockValue | AnyWordValue
        | DatatypeValue | NativeValue | FunctionValue | OpValue | ObjectValue
        | ErrorValue | JavaObjectValue | MapValue | BitsetValue
        | ModuleValue | PortValue | StructValue

    -- The concrete datatype this value reports to TYPE?. Each variant
    -- narrows this to the datatypes it can carry.
    datatype: Datatype

    -- Typeset membership, as R3-Alpha defines the standard typesets.
    is_any_string: datatype in {string, file, url, email, tag}
    is_any_block: datatype in {block, paren, path, set_path, get_path, lit_path}
    is_any_word: datatype in {word, set_word, get_word, lit_word, refinement, issue}
    is_any_path: datatype in {path, set_path, get_path, lit_path}
    is_series: is_any_string or is_any_block or datatype = binary
    is_number: datatype in {integer, decimal, percent}
    is_scalar: datatype in {integer, decimal, percent, money, char, pair, tuple, time, date}
    is_any_function: datatype in {native, function, op}

    -- Conditional truth. Only NONE and logic FALSE are false; everything
    -- else is true, including zero, the empty string and the empty block.
    -- UNSET is neither: using it as a condition raises an error, which is
    -- why this derivation is guarded by is_conditional.
    is_conditional: datatype != unset
    is_truthy: datatype != none and kind != LogicValue

    @guidance
        -- LogicValue overrides is_truthy through its own truth field. The
        -- derivation here covers every other variant, all of which are true.
}

-- A set of character codes, held as bits so membership is a lookup
-- rather than a search. Not a series: it has no position and no order,
-- and FIND answers whether something is in it rather than where.
-- A path reads and writes one bit of a set. `b/(#"a")` answers true or
-- false, and `b/(#"a"): true` puts that character in the set.
--
-- Reading answers a logic and never none, thus a character the set does not
-- hold answers false. That matters because the two look alike in a
-- condition and part company under NONE? and under LOGIC?.
--
-- Writing minds the complement flag and inverts what true means. A
-- complemented set holds every character its octets do not name, thus to
-- put a character in one, the octet for that character must be cleared.
-- `Set_Bits(ser, select, BITS_NOT(ser) ? !t : t)` in the C, and the
-- inversion is easy to miss because it changes nothing for an ordinary set.
--
-- Rebol's own url-parser needs the write. It copies the URI set and then
-- adds the percent sign to it, so that an already-encoded URL is left
-- alone.
variant BitsetValue : Value {
    bits: List<Integer>         -- one octet per eight codes, lowest first

    -- Whether the set holds every character its octets do not name.
    is_complemented: Boolean
}

-- A map!, written #[key: value ...] and made with `make map!`.
--
-- Not a series: it has no position, so NEXT and FIRST mean nothing on
-- one, and LENGTH? counts pairs rather than items. Reading a key that is
-- not there gives NONE rather than raising, which is the opposite of an
-- object and the same as a series position past the end: a map is asked
-- about keys it may not have, and an object is asked for fields it should.
variant MapValue : Value {
    storage: MapStorage

    -- Two maps are equal when they hold the same keys with equal values.
    -- Order of insertion is not part of it.
}

variant UnsetValue : Value {
    -- The absence of a value. Distinct from NONE, which is a value that
    -- means "nothing". UNSET is what an unset word holds and what a
    -- function returns when it returns nothing.
}

variant NoneValue : Value {
    -- The single NONE value. All NONE values are equal.
}

variant LogicValue : Value {
    truth: Boolean
}

variant IntegerValue : Value {
    magnitude: Integer          -- 64-bit signed, per R3-Alpha
}

variant DecimalValue : Value {
    -- Covers decimal! and percent!, which share IEEE 754 double
    -- representation and differ only in how MOLD prints them.
    quantity: Decimal
}

-- Amounts keep the scale they were written with, because money! is not
-- normalised: $1.50 and $1.5 hold different scales. Whether they are
-- nonetheless equal is an open question below, and it is exactly the
-- distinction BigDecimal draws between equals and compareTo.
--
-- The range is bounded and both bounds matter. Rebol's `deci` struct packs
-- a money into ninety-six bits: one sign bit, an eight-bit signed
-- exponent, and a whole-number significand across three fields totalling
-- eighty-seven bits. So a money carries at most twenty-six significant
-- digits and its power of ten runs from -128 to 127, and arithmetic that
-- leaves either bound raises overflow rather than widening.
--
-- Doubling $1 five hundred and eight times is the assertion that pins it:
-- the answer is $83798799562141231872337704e127, and doubling once more
-- raises. An implementation on an unbounded decimal computes the same
-- five-hundred-and-ninth value happily, which is the failure that looks
-- like success.
--
-- Ninety-six bits is also the width of the binary form, which is why
-- `to money! #{0F}` is $15 and `to-binary $15` is twelve bytes.
variant MoneyValue : Value {
    amount: Decimal
    scale: Integer              -- digits after the point, as written
    currency: String?           -- absent for the bare $1.50 form; 3 chars

    -- The whole number the digits spell, without the point. This is what
    -- the significand fields hold, and what the bound applies to.
    significand: absolute(amount) * power(10, scale)

    -- The power of ten the significand is multiplied by. Rebol's exponent
    -- field is the negation of the scale.
    exponent: 0 - scale
}

variant CharValue : Value {
    codepoint: Integer
}

-- A coordinate pair, written 40x40.
--
-- Both halves are decimals, not integers, which is not what the spelling
-- suggests: 1x2 reads back as 1x2 but FIRST 1x2 is 1.0, and 1.5x2 is a
-- legal pair. R3 was asked and this is what it answers. Molding hides the
-- point again when a half is whole, so the decimal nature is visible only
-- when you take a half out or divide: 1x2 / 2 is 0.5x1.
-- The halves are single precision rather than double, which is the fact
-- that explains every surprising thing a pair does. Rebol declares them
-- `REBD32`, a C float, so a half carries about seven significant digits
-- and nothing more.
--
-- Three consequences, none of them guessable from the spelling:
--
-- A large whole number loses its low digits. 2147483647x1 molds as
-- 2.147484e9x1, and 2147483647 and 1073741823 land on the same pair half
-- once halved, which is why `2147483647x2147483647 / 2` equals
-- `1073741823x1073741823`.
--
-- A half above about 3.4e38 becomes infinite rather than staying large.
-- `as-pair 1e300 -1e300` molds as `1.#INFx-1.#INF`, and the infinity is a
-- value the pair keeps rather than an error. A pair is the one datatype
-- here that holds an infinity as a matter of course.
--
-- A fraction is stored to single precision and read back at double, so
-- taking a half out shows the rounding: `first 0.1x0.2` is
-- 0.100000001490116 and not 0.1. Molding hides it again, because a pair
-- half molds to seven digits.
variant PairValue : Value {
    x: Decimal      -- narrowed to single precision on construction
    y: Decimal

    -- Read as a path, a pair answers to two spellings for each half: the
    -- name and the position. p/x and p/1 are the same question. A third
    -- spelling, p/area, is derived rather than stored.
    area: absolute(x * y)
}

-- Up to twelve octets, written 100.150.150, with twelve zeros behind
-- whatever was written.
--
-- Two lengths, and nearly everything about a tuple follows from the gap
-- between them. The kept length is how many octets were written down and
-- can be anything from none to twelve. The shown length is never below
-- three, so a tuple of one octet still reads and molds as three.
--
-- The octets past the kept length are zeros rather than absent, which is
-- what makes 1.2.3 equal to 1.2.3.0. The two are equal and are not the
-- same tuple, because only the length tells them apart.
variant TupleValue : Value {
    segments: List<Integer>     -- what was written; 0 to 12 octets

    segment_count: segments.count

    -- What LENGTH? answers, and how far a path may read.
    shown_count: max(segments.count, 3)
}

variant TimeValue : Value {
    nanoseconds: Integer        -- signed; may exceed 24 hours
}

variant DateValue : Value {
    year: Integer
    month: Integer
    day: Integer
    time_of_day: TimeValue?
    zone_minutes: Integer?      -- offset from UTC, present only with a time
}

variant AnyStringValue : Value {
    string_type: StringDatatype
    storage: StringStorage
    index: Integer              -- 1-based position; storage.length + 1 is the tail
}

variant BinaryValue : Value {
    storage: BinaryStorage
    index: Integer
}

-- A position into an image, counted in pixels rather than in bytes.
--
-- `VAL_INDEX` is the pixel and `QUAD_SKIP` turns it into the byte offset, so an
-- image navigates exactly as a block does and its width survives being skipped
-- into: `at img 3` is the third pixel of the same 2x2 image, not a 1x1 one.
variant ImageValue : Value {
    storage: ImageStorage
    index: Integer              -- 1-based pixel; storage.length + 1 is the tail
}

-- An opaque thing the runtime owns, which a script can only pass around.
--
-- `c-handle.c` says what it grew into: "initially handles were just user
-- transparent pointer holders. But it seems to be more useful to have it as a
-- more advanced type, with possibility to register a function for releasing
-- resources from GC when handle value is no more needed."
--
-- There are two kinds and nearly everything depends on which. A *function*
-- handle wraps something callable -- a codec dispatcher, an extension entry
-- point. A *context* handle owns a resource with a lifetime -- a cipher's key
-- schedule, a port's device state. Only a context handle publishes anything
-- about itself, and only a context handle can be released.
--
-- Nothing makes one: the Make column of `boot/types.reb` is a dash and
-- `MT_Handle` is `return FALSE` and nothing else. So every handle a script sees
-- was handed to it, and in a build with no ciphers and no extension API the only
-- ones are the two codecs `Init_Codecs` registers at boot.
--
-- Three comparisons and they disagree with each other on purpose. `same?` is
-- identity. `equal?` needs *both* sides to be context handles of one kind, so a
-- function handle is equal to nothing at all, including itself. And the ordering
-- puts every context handle before every function one -- which is what FIND and
-- SORT actually use, because they go through `Cmp_Value` rather than through the
-- equality dispatch.
variant HandleValue : Value {
    type_name: Text             -- a word: `codec`, `rc4`, `aes`
    kind: HandleKind
    identity: Integer           -- what `same?` compares and the ordering sorts on
    payload: Value?             -- what the runtime put behind it
}

-- Which of the two a handle is. `HANDLE_FUNCTION = 0` and
-- `HANDLE_CONTEXT = 1 << 2` in sys-value.h.
enum HandleKind {
    function | context
}

-- A thing that happened, waiting to be answered: a click, a key, a connection
-- opening, a file dropped on a window.
--
-- Not a series and not a container. The C keeps one in a single value cell of
-- twelve bytes -- "events are kept compact in order to fit into normal 128 bit
-- value cells", says `t-event.c` -- and every field a script reads is unpacked
-- from those bytes rather than stored as itself. So an event has two arms and
-- MAKE, and nothing else: `REBTYPE(Event)` handles A_MAKE and A_TO and sends
-- everything else to `Trap_Action`.
--
-- The packing is where the surprises live, and there are three.
--
-- The type is an *index* into `system/catalog/event-types`, so what a type word
-- means is decided by a block a script can read, and index 0 reads back as none
-- rather than as the word `ignore` that sits there.
--
-- `offset`, `key` and `code` are one four-byte field seen three ways, and two
-- flags say which way to read it. An offset packs two signed sixteen-bit halves
-- into it and raises HAS_XY; a key or a code puts a number in it and raises
-- HAS_CODE instead. Writing either takes the other's flag down, so HAS_XY and
-- HAS_CODE are never both up -- which is why an event with a key has no offset.
-- Each half of an offset is a signed short, and `Float_Int16` raises
-- `out-of-range` rather than truncating anything that will not fit in one.
--
-- And `port`, `gob` and `data` share one slot, with the model saying which is
-- there. A gob and a window are the same field under two names.
variant EventValue : Value {
    type_index: Integer         -- into system/catalog/event-types; 0 reads as none
    flags: List<EventFlag>
    model: EventModel
    data: Integer               -- an offset's two halves, or a key, or a code
    attached: Value?            -- the port, object or gob the model names
}

-- What an event's flags byte can say. `EVF_*` in reb-event.h.
--
-- Three of these a script can read back through `e/flags`: double, control and
-- shift. The rest are the C's own bookkeeping, and two of them decide how the
-- data field is read.
enum EventFlag {
    copied | has_xy | double | control | shift | has_data | has_code | alt
}

-- Which kind of thing the shared slot holds. `EVM_*` in reb-event.h.
--
-- Reading `e/port` goes through this: a GUI event answers
-- `system/ports/event`, a port event answers the port it holds, an object event
-- answers the object, and a device event has to reach through an I/O request to
-- find one.
enum EventModel {
    device | port | object | gui | callback | midi | console
}

-- A position into a gob's pane, which is what makes a gob a series.
--
-- The index walks the children rather than anything about the gob itself, so
-- `next gob` is the same gob standing at its second child. That is why the gob's
-- own fields are read through a path and never through a position.
variant GobValue : Value {
    storage: GobStorage
    index: Integer              -- 1-based child; storage.length + 1 is the tail
}

-- The layout of a C record: field names, each declaring one scalar type.
--
-- Construction is the whole of it, and that is deliberate rather than
-- unfinished-for-now. `#(struct! [a [uint8!]])` reads back as a value
-- STRUCT? affirms, which is what Rebol's own lexer test asks for and all it
-- asks for. Reading a field, writing one, molding the layout back out and
-- handing a record to a foreign library are all absent, because every one
-- of them is a foreign-function interface in disguise and JEBOL offers
-- none.
--
-- What that leaves out is worth naming, because a real R3 does more with
-- the same spelling. It carries the field values beside the layout, which
-- is why it molds as two blocks -- `#(struct! [a [uint8!]] [a: 0])` -- and
-- why LENGTH? of one answers the record's width in bytes. It reads an array
-- count inside the type block, `[uint8! [2]]`. And it accepts the aliases
-- `boot/words.reb` lists beside the canonical names -- float!, double!,
-- byte!, single!, i8!, u8! and the rest. None of the three is read here, so
-- a layout a real R3 accepts can be a malformed construct to JEBOL.
variant StructValue : Value {
    -- The block the construct carried, held exactly as it was written:
    -- pairs of a field word and a one-element block naming its type.
    layout: Value

    -- The type each of those pairs declared, in the order they were written.
    declared_field_types: List<StructFieldType>
}

-- The scalar types a struct field may declare, spelled as the canonical
-- names in `boot/words.reb`. Thirteen, and a field naming anything else
-- makes the whole layout a malformed construct.
enum StructFieldType {
    int8 | int16 | int32 | int64
    | uint8 | uint16 | uint32 | uint64
    | float32 | float64
    | pointer | word | rebval
}

variant AnyBlockValue : Value {
    block_type: BlockDatatype
    storage: BlockStorage
    index: Integer
}

variant AnyWordValue : Value {
    word_type: WordDatatype
    spelling: String            -- case preserved as written
    canonical: String           -- lowercased, the form used for comparison
    binding: Context?           -- absent when the word is unbound
}

variant DatatypeValue : Value {
    represents: Datatype        -- the value of integer!, string! and friends
}

variant NativeValue : Value {
    native_name: String
    parameters: List<Parameter>
}

-- An infix operator. Always exactly two arguments, the first of which
-- comes from the value already produced to its left rather than from the
-- position after it. Everything else about applying one is ordinary.
variant OpValue : Value {
    op_name: String
    underlying: Value           -- the native! or function! it dispatches to
}

-- A function, and a closure, which is the same value with one flag set.
--
-- The two datatypes differ in nothing a caller passes or receives; they
-- differ in what a call's own frame is. A function's frame is its private
-- word list, so CONTEXT? of a word bound into a running function answers the
-- function itself -- which is how a body reaches its own SPEC-OF from
-- inside. A closure's frame is a real object, so CONTEXT? of a word bound
-- into one answers that object.
--
-- Modelled as a flag rather than as two variants because everything else
-- about them is identical, and because something depends on the difference:
-- USE builds its scope as a closure's frame, so the words it introduces live
-- in an object a script can reach rather than in a word list it cannot.
variant FunctionValue : Value {
    spec: Value                 -- the block! the function was defined with
    body: Value                 -- the block! the function evaluates
    parameters: List<Parameter>
    closed_over: Context

    -- True for a closure, false for an ordinary function.
    is_closure: Boolean
}

variant ObjectValue : Value {
    context: Context
}

-- A module: a context that carries a header saying what it is called, what
-- version it is, and which of its words the rest of the world may see.
--
-- An object underneath, and R3's is the same object with one extra field.
-- What makes it a module rather than an object is that header, because the
-- header is what decides which names escape. A module whose header exports
-- three words puts three words into the library and keeps the rest to
-- itself, and a name it holds privately cannot collide with a library
-- function of the same spelling.
--
-- That collision is why this datatype is here rather than deferred again.
-- Rebol's own JSON codec is a module holding a parse rule named `exp` and
-- another named `stack`. Loaded flat, both replace the library functions
-- of those names, and the failure is silent: the word still answers, it
-- just answers a block.
variant ModuleValue : Value {
    context: Context            -- the words the module defines
    header: ObjectValue         -- name, version, type, options, exports

    -- The words the module publishes. Every other word it defines is
    -- private to it. Absent from the header means nothing is published.
    exported_names: List<String>
}

-- A port: something outside the interpreter that a script reads and writes.
--
-- An object underneath, like a module, and again the difference is what the
-- extra field is for. A port's is an actor, and the actor is what makes one
-- verb reach every kind of thing a script can open: reading a console port
-- and reading a file port are the same call to READ and two different
-- actors.
variant PortValue : Value {
    context: Context            -- spec, scheme, actor, state and the rest
    is_open: Boolean
}

-- A value that belongs to the host rather than to REBOL. REBOL code can
-- hold one, pass it around and reach through it; the host can hold REBOL
-- values in the same way. It has no REBOL spelling, so nothing the reader
-- produces is ever a java-object!, and MOLD cannot print one that reads
-- back.
-- A held Java null is not NONE. NONE is a REBOL value meaning nothing; a
-- Java null is the host's absence, and conflating the two would make a
-- round trip through the host lossy.
variant JavaObjectValue : Value {
    class_name: String          -- the runtime class, for TYPE? and MOLD
    is_host_null: Boolean       -- a held Java null, distinct from NONE
}

-- An error, which is a value like any other until something tries to
-- use it.
--
-- It is read through paths, and that is not a convenience: it is how
-- REBOL code asks which failure happened. `all [error? e: try [...]
-- e/id = 'expect-arg]` is the idiom Rebol's own test suite uses
-- throughout, and an implementation whose errors are opaque cannot run
-- any of it. A hundred and seventy-seven of JEBOL's failing assertions
-- reach for one of these fields.
--
-- The eight fields are fixed and in this order, because WORDS-OF
-- reports them and code walks the result. TYPE and ID are words rather
-- than strings, so they compare with a lit-word and not with a quoted
-- string. ARG1 to ARG3 carry whatever the failure was about -- the word
-- that had no value, the argument of the wrong datatype -- and are none
-- when the failure had nothing to name. A field an error has not got
-- raises rather than answering none, because an error either has that
-- field or the code asking is wrong.
variant ErrorValue : Value {
    code: Integer               -- the number R3 gives this failure
    category: ErrorCategory     -- read as TYPE, and a word: Math, Script
    error_id: String            -- read as ID, and a word: no-value, zero-divide
    message: String
    arg1: Value?                -- what the failure was about, if anything
    arg2: Value?
    arg3: Value?
    near: Value?                -- the block fragment being evaluated
    where_word: String?         -- the function the error was raised in

    -- The order WORDS-OF reports, which code walks.
    field_order: List<String> = [
        "code", "type", "id", "arg1", "arg2", "arg3", "near", "where"]
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- R3-Alpha integers are 64-bit signed. The bounds are stated here so
    -- the loader and arithmetic can both refer to one definition.
    max_integer: Integer = 9_223_372_036_854_775_807
    min_integer: Integer = -9_223_372_036_854_775_808

    -- A tuple keeps up to 12 octets and never shows fewer than 3.
    min_tuple_segments: Integer = 3
    max_tuple_segments: Integer = 12
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A series index is a position, and a position runs from the head to one
-- past the last element. One past the last element is the tail, which is
-- a legal position to hold but not to read from.
invariant BlockIndexInBounds {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = AnyBlockValue
            implies value.index >= 1 and value.index <= value.storage.length + 1
}

invariant StringIndexInBounds {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = AnyStringValue
            implies value.index >= 1 and value.index <= value.storage.length + 1
}

invariant BinaryIndexInBounds {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = BinaryValue
            implies value.index >= 1 and value.index <= value.storage.length + 1
}

-- The datatype a value reports must belong to the family its variant
-- represents. Without this a value could claim to be a string! while
-- carrying block storage.
invariant StringDatatypeMatchesVariant {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = AnyStringValue implies value.is_any_string
}

invariant BlockDatatypeMatchesVariant {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = AnyBlockValue implies value.is_any_block
}

invariant WordDatatypeMatchesVariant {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = AnyWordValue implies value.is_any_word
}

-- A struct exists only where every field of its layout declared one of the
-- thirteen scalar types. A layout where one did not is refused by the reader
-- as a malformed construct rather than built into a value with an unreadable
-- field, so there is no such thing as a struct with a field nobody can name.
--
-- Worth stating because it is what makes STRUCT? worth asking. A value that
-- answers it has a layout that could be laid over bytes, whether or not
-- anything here does the laying, and a caller can rely on that much without
-- having to check the block itself.
invariant StructFieldsDeclareOneOfTheThirteenScalarTypes {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = StructValue
            implies value.declared_field_types.count = fields_in(value.layout)
}

-- Word comparison is case-insensitive, so every word carries the
-- lowercased form it compares by alongside the spelling it prints as.
invariant WordCanonicalIsLowercase {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = AnyWordValue implies value.canonical = lowercase(value.spelling)
}

-- Two slots in one context cannot claim the same name, because a lookup
-- must resolve to exactly one slot.
invariant ContextSlotsAreUnique {
    for first in ContextSlots:
        for second in ContextSlots:
            first != second and first.context = second.context
                implies first.canonical != second.canonical
}

-- A module cannot publish a word it has not got. An export naming nothing
-- would put a slot holding UNSET into the library, and a library word that
-- answers UNSET reports a different failure from one that was never
-- defined.
invariant ExportedNamesAreHeld {
    for module in ModuleValues:
        for name in module.exported_names:
            is_known_in(module.context, name)
}

-- Tuples are bounded in both length and element range.
--
-- The floor is on what a tuple shows and not on what it keeps, so a
-- tuple of one octet is legal and shows as three.
-- Twenty-six digits and no more, because the significand has eighty-seven
-- bits to hold them in.
invariant MoneySignificandFitsInEightySevenBits {
    for money in MoneyValues:
        money.significand < power(10, 26)
}

-- And the power of ten fits in a signed byte.
invariant MoneyExponentFitsInASignedByte {
    for money in MoneyValues:
        money.exponent >= -128 and money.exponent <= 127
}

-- A pair half is stored to single precision. Nothing widens it back: a
-- half read out is the single precision value shown as a double, which is
-- why `first 0.1x0.2` is 0.100000001490116.
invariant PairHalvesAreSinglePrecision {
    for pair in PairValues:
        pair.x = narrowed_to_single(pair.x)
            and pair.y = narrowed_to_single(pair.y)
}

invariant TupleShape {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = TupleValue
            implies value.segment_count >= 0
                and value.segment_count <= config.max_tuple_segments
}

invariant TupleShowsAtLeastThree {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = TupleValue
            implies value.shown_count >= config.min_tuple_segments
}

invariant TupleSegmentsAreOctets {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = TupleValue
            implies value.segments.all(segment => segment >= 0 and segment <= 255)
}

-- Characters are Unicode scalar values. R3-Alpha stops at the Basic
-- Multilingual Plane, but the JVM's own char is UTF-16, so JEBOL admits
-- the full scalar range and excludes the surrogate block.
invariant CharIsUnicodeScalar {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = CharValue
            implies value.codepoint >= 0 and value.codepoint <= 1_114_111
}

-- A date carries a zone only when it carries a time. A bare date has
-- neither, because there is no instant to offset.
invariant ZoneRequiresTime {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = DateValue
            implies (value.zone_minutes != null implies value.time_of_day != null)
}

-- A refinement argument must follow a refinement that exists on the same
-- function, otherwise the argument can never be supplied.
invariant RefinementArgumentsHaveOwners {
    for value in Values:
        value.kind = FunctionValue
            implies value.parameters.all(p =>
                p.kind = refinement_arg implies p.owning_refinement != null)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Resolved, and corrected. An earlier note here claimed R3 molds the
-- shortest representation that reads back, on the reasoning that it must
-- differ from REBOL 2. Running a real R3 (3.22.1, arm64) says otherwise,
-- and the Core guide was right all along.
--
-- A decimal molds to at most fifteen significant digits, always with a
-- decimal point, and in exponent form only when the decimal exponent is
-- 15 or more, or -7 or less. Pinned by running one:
--
--   0.1 + 0.2  ->  0.3                    not 0.30000000000000004
--   10 / 3     ->  3.33333333333333       fifteen digits
--   1.0        ->  1.0                    the point is never dropped
--   1e14       ->  100000000000000.0      plain up to exponent 14
--   1e15       ->  1.0e15                 exponent from 15
--   1e-6       ->  0.000001               plain down to exponent -6
--   1e-7       ->  1.0e-7                 exponent from -7
--
-- So Java's Double.toString is the wrong tool: it gives seventeen digits
-- where fifteen are wanted. The rounding is ours to do.

-- Resolved: money! is an arbitrary-precision decimal carrying a currency
-- designator, held as a Decimal here and as a BigDecimal in the code.
--
-- The earlier note in this file said R3-Alpha stored money as a lossy
-- decimal. That was wrong, and it was REBOL 2 being described. R3-Alpha's
-- money! is a 128-bit type with 26 significant digits and an exponent
-- from -128 to +127, and it is deliberately not normalised, so $1.50
-- keeps its trailing zero rather than becoming $1.5. A currency
-- designator of up to three characters sits alongside.
--
-- That maps onto BigDecimal closely: 26 digits is a MathContext, and
-- BigDecimal preserves scale, which is what non-normalised means. Storing
-- integer minor units instead would have been wrong twice over, because
-- 26 significant digits do not fit and a bare $1.50 names no currency
-- whose minor unit could be looked up.

-- Resolved against a real R3: $1.50 = $1.5 and $1.50 == $1.5 are both
-- true, while mold $1.50 still gives "$1.50". The scale is kept for
-- printing and ignored for comparing, under the loose and the strict
-- operator alike. So BigDecimal.compareTo is what both mean, and
-- BigDecimal.equals is what neither means.

-- Resolved against a real R3: $10 / 3 gives
-- $3.3333333333333333333333333, which is 26 significant digits. The same
-- width as the type, so division needs no rule of its own.

-- Resolved: integer arithmetic always raises on overflow, never wraps.
-- R3-Alpha is inconsistent about this and the JVM wraps silently, which
-- is the worst of the options because a wrong answer looks like a right
-- one. Costs a checked operation per arithmetic native.

-- Resolved against a real R3: the loose = ignores case for every
-- string-like type and not only for string!. %A.txt = %a.txt is true and
-- <A> = <a> is true, while == minds case for all of them.

-- Resolved against a real R3: binding is a snapshot, not a subscription.
-- Binding a block to an object binds the words the object knows at that
-- moment; extending the object afterwards does not make an already-bound
-- word see the new slot. Demonstrated by binding [a b] to an object with
-- only a, extending it with b, and finding the bound b still has no value.
--
-- So JEBOL keeps the limit, and it is not really a limit: it is what makes
-- a bound block mean the same thing later that it meant when it was bound.

-- Resolved against a real R3: a bound word names the context that holds
-- its slot, never a descendant that only reaches it through a parent.
-- Contexts nest here, so binding a block into an inner scope could plainly
-- have marked every word with that scope. It must not: BIND asked for the
-- home of a word answers wherever that word lives, and code that adds a
-- name there expects it to outlive the block being bound. REBOL's own
-- boot writes its six reflector functions exactly that way, inside a USE
-- whose scope disappears immediately afterwards.

-- Resolved against a real R3: a series value keeps its own position, so
-- shortening the storage underneath one leaves it standing past the end.
-- Take three items off a four-item block that something else holds at
-- position four, and that other value is stranded: it reads as empty,
-- answers true to TAIL? and EMPTY?, and PAST? exists to ask about
-- exactly this.
--
-- It is an ordinary state rather than an error, and every operation has
-- to cope with it. JEBOL computed the remaining items as a sublist from
-- the position, and a stranded one threw an IllegalArgumentException
-- straight out of the interpreter -- or, for a string, a
-- NegativeArraySizeException from building a buffer of negative size.
-- Both are the kind of failure spec/embed.allium says cannot happen.

-- Resolved: an interpreter instance is single-threaded and owns every
-- value reachable from it. Concurrency comes from running many instances.
-- Series aliasing therefore needs no synchronisation, because nothing
-- shares storage across a thread boundary. See docs/decisions.md item 4.

-- Resolved: the lexical types stay lexical, as in R3-Alpha. A tuple may
-- hold 999.999.999 and an email is a shape rather than a checked address.
-- The invariants below constrain what the reader can build, not what a
-- caller may construct through conversion. See docs/decisions.md item 7.

open question "A malformed map literal such as #[none] is refused by the reader with a syntax malconstruct, where a real Rebol refuses it with a script invalid-arg. Rebol reads the brackets into a block and builds the map afterwards; JEBOL builds it while reading. Is the reader the right place to build a map at all, or should #[...] read as a block that something later turns into one?"

open question "Should a struct! ever be more than its layout here? Everything a real R3 does with one past construction exists to serve a foreign-function interface: the field values are there so a record can be filled and handed to a C library, the byte width is there so the library can be told how much to read, and the array counts are there because C arrays are laid out that way. An interpreter embedded in a host application already has a way to reach foreign code -- the host calls in and hands values across -- so a second one modelled on C's memory layout would be a parallel road to the same place, and a worse one, since it would put pointer arithmetic in a language that has none. The argument the other way is that a script that reads or writes a struct field is not asking for an FFI at all: it is asking to lay a record over a binary, which is a reasonable thing to want when parsing a file format, and STRUCT is the only place REBOL offers it. So the question is really whether struct! is a foreign-call fixture or a binary-layout tool, and only the second is worth building here."

open question "Nothing may cross between interpreter instances yet, because a block carries its storage and its bindings with it. Is passing values between instances supported at all, and if so is it by deep copy, or by a handle that only the owning instance may follow?"

-- Resolved: integer! crosses as a long and block! as a List. Anything
-- without an obvious counterpart stays a REBOL value the host asks about
-- rather than being guessed at.

-- Resolved against a real R3: a bitset! is a set of character codes,
-- held as bits so membership is a lookup rather than a search. FIND
-- answers a logic on one rather than a position, because there is no
-- position in a set: it is asked whether something is in it.
--
-- It molds as construction syntax carrying the bits, so
-- `make bitset! "ab"` molds as `#(bitset! #{...60})` with the sixth and
-- seventh bits of the thirteenth byte set, those being 97 and 98.
--
-- Wanted for PARSE, where a bitset is how a rule says "any of these
-- characters" without listing them as alternatives, and by two of
-- Rebol's own library files.

-- Resolved against a real R3: comparing series folds case all the way
-- down, so `equal? ["a"] ["A"]` is true and not only `equal? "a" "A"`.
-- JEBOL folded case at the top level and compared nested values
-- strictly, which is the kind of split nobody writes on purpose and
-- nothing catches until a block holds a string.

-- Resolved against a real R3: two objects are equal when they hold the
-- same fields with equal values, whatever contexts they were built in.
-- `equal? #(object! [a: 1]) #(object! [a: 1])` is true. JEBOL compared
-- objects by the identity of their context, so two objects built the same
-- way were never equal, and nothing noticed until construction syntax
-- made it easy to build the same object twice.
--
-- The `self` field is left out of the comparison, as it is left out of
-- molding, because every object has one and it points back at the object.
-- Counting it would make the comparison recurse for ever.

-- Resolved: MOLD prints #[java-object! <class>], which is deliberately not
-- readable. The round-trip invariant is scoped to values the reader could
-- have produced, and nothing the reader produces is ever a java-object!.
-- Refusing to mold one at all would make debugging worse for no gain.

-- Resolved against a real R3, and it is not what a reader of this file
-- would guess. The values with no literal spelling mold as construction
-- syntax rather than as the words that name them:
--
--     mold none    is  "_"
--     mold true    is  "#(true)"
--     mold false   is  "#(false)"
--     mold unset   is  "#(unset)"
--
-- NONE is the odd one: a single underscore, not `#(none)`, though
-- `#(none)` still reads. Molding these as the bare words `none` and
-- `true` is what JEBOL did, and it broke round-tripping in the direction
-- nobody checks: `true` reads back as a word bound to a function rather
-- than as the logic it came from.

-- Resolved: every host throwable becomes an error! at the boundary, so no
-- exception crosses into REBOL as an exception. A host exception caught by
-- REBOL and passed back arrives as an error value rather than the original
-- throwable. That loss is the price of the guarantee that a script can
-- catch anything a script can cause.

-- Resolved: JEBOL makes no promise about host objects. An instance owns
-- its REBOL values; a Java object it holds may be shared and mutated by
-- anyone. This is the one hole in the isolation story and it is
-- deliberate, because closing it would mean copying or freezing everything
-- crossing the boundary. It must be stated where a host developer hands an
-- object over, not left in a decisions file.
